 {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186613135} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186613167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186613181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186650983}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Vector Lies in the Null Space of a Given Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119813 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119624} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9171c6fb-ad0b-538f-b982-a2502a20e253" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049151}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186613178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186613180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186650946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186650972}] :knowledge-point/title "Solve a Variable in a Vector Lying in the Null Space of a Given Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119814 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119625} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "465ce2b7-352e-5050-a5e4-0753fbacebf5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048445}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186613179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186650931}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Null Space of a Given Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121032 :tutorial/content "The **real coordinate space** (of dimension $n$) is the set of all possible column vectors of height $n,$ whose components are real numbers. Symbolically, we represent the real coordinate space of dimension $n$ as $\\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFor example, the following vectors are in $\\mathbb{R}, \\mathbb{R}^2,$ $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ and $\\mathbb{R}^5,$ respectively: \n\n$$\n6,\\qquad \n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}\\sqrt{4} \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]},\n\t\\qquad\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 8 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]},\n\t\\qquad\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}5 \\\\ - 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ - 7\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nWe've already encountered several operations on vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ or $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ namely, vector addition and subtraction, scalar multiplication, and the dot product. All of these operations work the same way in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "084d7eb8-e6bd-5678-98b5-149f18e61a8b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3182 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121033 :tutorial/content "In general, if we have two vectors $\\mathbf{u},\\mathbf{v}\\in\\mathbb{R}^n$ and a scalar $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb{R},$ then our vector operations are defined as follows:\n\n1\\. The **sum/difference** of the two vectors is defined as\n  $$\n\\mathbf{u} \\pm \\mathbf{v} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ \\vdots  \\\\ {u}_{n}\\end{array} \\right]} \\pm {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ \\vdots  \\\\ {v}_{n}\\end{array} \\right]} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{u}_{1} \\pm  {v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\pm  {v}_{2} \\\\ \\vdots  \\\\ {u}_{n} \\pm  {v}_{n}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\n2\\. **Scalar multiplication** of a vector is defined as\n  $$\n\\quad \\lambda \\mathbf{u} =\\lambda \\cdot {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ \\vdots  \\\\ {u}_{n}\\end{array} \\right]} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}\\lambda  \\cdot  {u}_{1} \\\\ \\lambda  \\cdot  {u}_{2} \\\\ \\vdots  \\\\ \\lambda  \\cdot  {u}_{n}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\n3\\. The **dot product** of two vectors is defined as\n\n  $$\n\\quad \\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{v} = \\underbrace{u_1 \\cdot v_1 + u_2 \\cdot v_2 + \\cdots + u_n \\cdot v_n}_{\\Large{n} \\,\\textrm{-terms}} = \\sum_{i=1}^n u_i v_i.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a465d6d9-270d-5cd3-98c8-a78c527b3558" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3183 :tutorial/title "General Rules"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121039 :tutorial/content "The **null space** of a matrix $A$ is the set of all the solutions of the equation $A\\mathbf{x}= \\mathbf{0},$ and is denoted by $\\textrm{Null}(A).$ \n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA={\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThe null space consists of all the solutions of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ For example, the vectors ${\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 3 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]},$ ${\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}6 \\\\ - 2\\end{array} \\right]},$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ all satisfy the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0},$ so they are all elements of the null space:\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 3 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}6 \\\\ - 2\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 9 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]} \\in \\textrm{Null}(A)\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3d284a1f-e3a9-5b48-97e4-d621a81b5237" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3204 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121062 :tutorial/content "To add two vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ we just need to add their corresponding components. To illustrate, consider the vectors $\\mathbf{a},\\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{R}^4,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ - 3 \\\\ 6\\end{array} \\right]}, \\quad \\quad \\mathbf{b}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTo compute $\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b},$ all we have to do is to add each component of the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ with the corresponding component of $\\mathbf{b}.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5+(-5) \\\\ 1+3 \\\\ -3+1 \\\\ 6+0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can only compute the sum, difference, or dot product of two vectors if both vectors have the same number of components. For example, we can't find the following sum, so we say it's *undefined*:\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]} +  {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 5\\end{array} \\right]} = {\\color{red}\\times} \\:(\\textrm{undefined})\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "34cc8b84-ed7a-54e2-995c-12a2b57b0ff0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3922 :tutorial/title "Adding Two Vectors"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121063 :tutorial/content "Let's again consider the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA={\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nTo find an expression for the null space using set-builder notation, we need to find all the solutions of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ Let's start by considering the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\, \\mathbf{0}]$ and finding its reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,0] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st column). The second column is not a pivot column.\n\nNow, we convert the reduced matrix back into a system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 0 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, $\\color{blue}x_1$ is a basic variable (it corresponds to the pivot column), while $\\color{red}x_2$ is a free variable (it corresponds to the non-pivot column). Because there is a free variable, the system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has infinitely many solutions. \n\nFrom the first equation we get ${\\color{blue}x_1}=-3{\\color{red}x_2},$ so the set of all the solutions of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ is\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A) = \n\t\\left\\{  {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 3 {\\color{red}{x}_{2}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{x}_{2}}\\end{array} \\right]} \\: : \\: {\\color{red}x_2} \\in\\mathbb{R} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It is not necessary to consider the full augmented matrix $[A \\,|\\, \\mathbf{0}]$ since the last column won't be changed during the process of reduction. Really, we just need to find the reduced row echelon form of the matrix $A$ and then set up the corresponding system." :tutorial/id #uuid "895e6e15-cec9-50e1-9bed-dff731650d72" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3943 :tutorial/title "Writing the Null Space using Set-Builder Notation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124508 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124317} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce5ddb87-f7c0-5bda-8d6e-ac1728dd2f99" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049723}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186630982}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of the Null Space of a Matrix Given the Number of Non-Pivot Columns"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124509 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124318} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f020b27f-1cd4-550b-a83a-2afbb7e5db72" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549053}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Dimension of the Null Space of Matrices Using Inspection"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124510 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124319} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3a0e3b9-931a-52bd-9ed9-b8001c8de67b" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446049}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Nullity of a Matrix Using Row Reduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124511 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124320} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "084a0a83-5ce6-5184-beb7-8ae7b4f1a799" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186746870} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323674} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673230}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124629 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124433} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb90e5b9-03fe-51cf-bc16-a293befacebc" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725790} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186627687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186627692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186662343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186662344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673124} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673150}] :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124630 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124434} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "879514d5-e127-5485-ab41-d459f6b5f379" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050996}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725791} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186627715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186662269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186662306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673185}] :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125778 :tutorial/content "We already learned that the rank of a matrix $A$ is the number of pivot columns in $A.$ Now, can we obtain any information from the *non-pivot* columns?\n\nThe answer is yes! The non-pivot columns help to find the dimension of the null space of a matrix $A,$ and here's how.\n\nConsider the matrix \n$$\nA = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{blue}3} & {\\color{red}5} & {\\color{blue}1} \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{blue}2} \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{blue}0}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ is already in row echelon form, where the $1$st and the $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd one is a *non-pivot* column. Now, to get the null space of $A,$ we need to find the solution of the system\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 {\\color{blue}{x}_{1}} + 5 {\\color{red}{x}_{2}} + {\\color{blue}{x}_{3}} = 0 \\\\ 2 {\\color{blue}{x}_{3}} = 0 \\\\ 0 = 0\\end{array} \\right. }\n\\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\color{blue}{x}_{1}} = - {\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{3}} {\\color{red}{x}_{2}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{x}_{3}} = 0\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nwhere $\\color{blue}x_1$ and ${\\color{blue}x_3}$ are basic variables and $\\color{red}x_2$ is a free variable. Hence, we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- {\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{3}} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}\\right\\}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ and, therefore, $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))={\\color{red}1}$.\n\nIn other words,\n\n$\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))$ is equal to the number of *non-pivot* columns in the corresponding row echelon form of $A$\n\nor, equivalently,\n\n$\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))$  is the number of *free variables* in the corresponding homogeneous system with the coefficient matrix $A.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7c75fc81-c464-5eea-bb66-37258ca64cb5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3520 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125779 :tutorial/content "The dimension of the null space of a matrix $A$ is often called the **nullity** of $A$. We will also use the following notation:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{nullity}(A) = \\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(A)).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e46fe003-9e24-5794-b139-5c2805d463c5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3521 :tutorial/title "The Nullity of a Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125780 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ invertible matrix $A.$ Remember that if $A$ is invertible, then the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has only the trivial solution $\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nLet's extract all possible information from this fact using the vector spaces related to the matrix, namely, the *column space* and the *null space*.\n\n- The fact that the unique solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ is $\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ tells us that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A)=\\{ \\mathbf{0} \\},\n$$\n\n or alternatively, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Null}(A)) = \\textrm{nullity}(A)=0.\n$$\n\n- Furthermore, if $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))=0$ then $A$ has no *non-pivot* columns, or equivalently, all the columns of $A$ are *pivot columns*. So \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Col}(A))=\\textrm{rank}(A)=n.\n$$\n\n- Finally, since $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ and its basis has $n$ linearly independent vectors, we can conclude that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Col}(A)=\\mathbb{R}^n.\n$$\n\nAll the results mentioned above can be summarized in the following theorem, called the **invertible matrix theorem**:\n\n*Let $A$ be an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n\n$$\nA \\textrm{ is invertible} \\qquad \\Leftrightarrow \\qquad \\left\\{\\; \\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={\\mathbb{R}}^{n} \\\\ & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{\\mathbf{0}\\} \\\\ & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned} \\right.\n$$\n\n" :tutorial/id #uuid "555ca00c-d37e-5c67-a658-69b087390920" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3522 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133781 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133590} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed5c8af7-e617-5e8c-be01-45a26eb17d4d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048849}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186599234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186605146}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthonormal"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133782 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133591} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26f8f84c-bb94-5f3c-a78e-343f9c0c1b1a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048445}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134220} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326836} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351544} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186599208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186599217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186599253}] :knowledge-point/title "Extending a Set to Form an Orthogonal Basis"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133837 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133652} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "baf333d2-6bd9-5d79-9c07-85856c81672a" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134518} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186605088}] :knowledge-point/title "Extending a Set to Form an Orthonormal Basis"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133843 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133658} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee1838df-f1d6-5bc4-908c-b96e2ad577c9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049951}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186599219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617838}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthogonal"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135045 :tutorial/content "A set of vectors ${U}=\\{ \\mathbf{u}_1, \\cdots, \\mathbf{u}_p \\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is an **orthogonal set** if each pair of distinct vectors from the set is orthogonal.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following set:\n\n$$\n{W}=\\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ - 4 \\\\ 7\\end{array} \\right]} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nIf we denote the vectors in our set ${W}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{w}_1, \\mathbf{w}_2, \\mathbf{w}_3 \\},$ we can check that ${W}$ is orthogonal by showing that \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's compute each dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}=3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 7=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}=(-1)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 7=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo indeed, ${W}$ is an orthogonal set.\n\nIf an orthogonal set is also a basis, then we call it an **orthogonal basis**. Here, $W$ is an orthogonal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ because it contains three linearly independent (you can check this) orthogonal vectors." :tutorial/id #uuid "dd93c4b2-6ec7-5a9c-a669-7979210393dd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3618 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135090 :tutorial/content "A set of vectors ${U}=\\{\\mathbf{u}_1, \\cdots, \\mathbf{u}_p \\}$ is an **orthonormal set** if it is an orthogonal set of *unit* vectors. Remember that $\\mathbf u_i$ is a unit vector if $\\Vert \\mathbf u_i \\Vert = 1,$ or equivalently, $\\mathbf u_i \\cdot \\mathbf u_i = 1.$\n\nAn orthonormal set that is also a basis, like the standard basis, is called an **orthonormal basis**. For instance, the standard basis $\\{\\mathbf{e}_1, \\mathbf{e}_2, \\mathbf{e}_3\\}$ of $\\mathbb R^3$ is an orthonormal basis because the vectors are orthogonal to each other and each vector in the set is a unit vector.\n\nThere are two more things to note about orthogonal sets:\n\n- Any orthogonal set of non-zero vectors is linearly independent.\n\n- An orthogonal set in $\\Bbb R^n$ cannot contain more than $n$ vectors. More precisely, if $V = \\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\dots, \\mathbf{v}_p\\}$ is an orthogonal set of non-zero vectors and $\\mathbf{v}_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^n,$ then we must have that $p\\leq n.$\n\n**Note:** To remember that an orthogonal set of non-zero vectors in $\\Bbb R^n$ cannot contain more than $n$ vectors, you can use the following intuitive mnemonic. There are only $n$ dimensions in $\\Bbb R^n,$ and each non-zero orthogonal vector has a $1$-dimensional span, meaning that it \"takes up\" $1$ dimension of $\\Bbb R^n.$ So, you can't \"fit\" more than $n$ orthogonal vectors in $\\Bbb R^n.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "85873149-e58a-571e-8f5f-08cb9460ee47" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4353 :tutorial/title "Orthonormal Sets and Orthonormal Bases"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152155 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151943} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39d79985-57fa-554a-b697-4bd6dd71cbe2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049723}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423620} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186615410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186615440} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186626778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186653551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186666079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186666147}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152156 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151944} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db27967e-f80c-587b-9b10-28f1a5518f57" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049422}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186615392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186615406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186626698}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix With Two Rows"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152157 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151945} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3bf9ea3-d57f-53f6-aa3c-eddffe1a8cfa" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050178}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536836} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186615396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186615407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186626715}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix For a Larger Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153352 :tutorial/content "Remember that the column space of a matrix is the span of its columns. For example, for the matrix\n\n$$\nA={\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 2 & - 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 5\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\n\nthe column space is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Col}(A) = \\text{Span} \\Bigg\\{ \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_1}, \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_2},\n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 5\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_3}\n\t\\Bigg\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, to find a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A),$ we can apply the usual procedure of converting the matrix $A$ to row echelon form and then picking the pivot columns from the original matrix $A.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "141672d1-fa24-513d-ba34-8cbf5a1d0b84" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3965 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153353 :tutorial/content "In the previous example, we computed a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A),$ where \n\n$$\nA={\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 2 & - 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 5\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nThe basis that we found previously was\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B} = \\Bigg\\{ \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_1}, \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_2} \n\t\\Bigg\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Col}(A) = \\textrm{Span}\\,\\mathcal B\n= \\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf a_1, \\mathbf a_2\\}\n\n= \n\\textrm{Span} \\Bigg\\{ \n\t\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]}, \n\t\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t\\Bigg\\}.\n$$\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{a}_3$ was not included in the basis because the $3$rd column of $A$ was not a pivot column. This means that $\\mathbf{a}_3$ can be written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$\n\nNow, what if we want to find the exact linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ that yields $\\mathbf{a}_3?$ That is to say, we want to find $x_1, x_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that \n\n$$\n{\\color{black}x_1} \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_1} + {\\color{rblack}x_2} \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_2} = \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 5\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_3}.\n$$\n\nThis is equivalent to a system with the augmented matrix\n$$\n\\left[\n\t{\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & - 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 5\\end{array}}\n\t\\right].\n$$\n\nIf we reduced this matrix to the *reduced* row echelon form, we get\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t{\\begin{array}{cc|c}\\bbox[3pt,lightgray]{ {\\color{blue}1}} & 0 & {\\color{red}3} \\\\ 0 & \\bbox[3pt,lightgray]{ {\\color{blue}1}} & {\\color{red}- 2}\\end{array}}\n\t\\right].\n$$\n\nFinally, the third column of the *reduced* matrix tells us that $x_1=3$ and $x_2=-2,$ meaning that\n$$\n\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 5\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_3} = {\\color{red}3}\\cdot \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_1} + ({\\color{red}-2})\\cdot \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_2}.\n$$\n\nIn general, if the $n$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express $\\mathbf{a}_n$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. \n\n**Watch out!** The matrix must be written in *reduced* row echelon form (with $1$'s for pivots and $0$'s above and below the pivots), not just in a row echelon form." :tutorial/id #uuid "cb82414a-0cb2-5363-9301-c8d79896bc22" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3966 :tutorial/title "Finding a Particular Linear Combination of Vectors"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184167 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184065} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bdb6bc9-47e2-5330-a1ee-270882f2aced" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186609025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186649877}] :knowledge-point/title "Proving Statements Using the Vector Space Axioms"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184196 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184094} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d9b62ae-7545-5d65-bcf7-74ab0e8662ec" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049737}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396592} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423671} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186649906}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That Are Not Vector Spaces"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185317 :tutorial/content "In previous lessons, we've seen how some sets equipped with addition and scalar multiplication operations exhibit properties similar to $\\mathbb R^n.$ \n\nIn this lesson, we will formally define what we mean by a **vector space.**\n\nFirst, let $V$ be a set equipped with the following two operations:\n\nVector addition: \n\nFor any two elements $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\in V,$ there is a unique element $\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y}$ that also lies in $V.$ We say that $V$ is **closed under vector addition.**\n\nScalar multiplication: \n\nFor any element $\\mathbf{x} \\in V$ and any number $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R},$ there is a unique element $\\alpha \\mathbf{x}$ that also lies in $V.$ We say that $V$ is **closed under scalar multiplication.**\n\nA set $V$ equipped with vector addition and scalar multiplication forms a **vector space** if it satisfies the following **vector space axioms**. \n\nAxioms of Vector Addition ($+$)\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A1]}$: $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y} =  \\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{x}$ for any $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\in V$; vector addition is **commutative**.\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A2]}$: $\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})+\\mathbf{z} =  \\mathbf{x}+(\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{z})$ for any $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y}, \\mathbf{z} \\in V$; vectors addition is **associative**.\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A3]}$: There exists a **zero-vector** $\\mathbf{0}$ such that $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x} + \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{x}$.\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A4]}$: For every $\\mathbf{x} \\in V,$ there exists $-\\mathbf{x} \\in V$ such that $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x} + (-\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{0}$; $-\\mathbf{x}$ is called the **negative** of $\\mathbf{x}$.\n\nAxioms of Scalar Multiplication ($\\cdot$)\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A5]}$: $\\color{blue}1 \\cdot \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{x}$ for any $\\mathbf{x} \\in V$; multiplying by $1$ does not change a vector.\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A6]}$: $\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{x}) = (\\alpha\\beta) \\mathbf{x}$ for any numbers $\\alpha,\\beta \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and any vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in V$.\n\nAxioms Connecting Vector Addition and Scalar Multiplication (the Distributive Laws)\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A7]}$: $\\color{blue}(\\alpha + \\beta)\\mathbf{x} = \\alpha \\mathbf{x} + \\beta \\mathbf{x}$ for any numbers $\\alpha,\\beta \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and any vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in V$.\n\n- $\\color{black}\\textrm{[A8]}$: $\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\mathbf{x} + \\mathbf{y}) = \\alpha \\mathbf{x} + \\alpha \\mathbf{y}$ for any number $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and any vectors $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\in V$.\n\nSo, a vector space is simply a collective term for all kinds of sets equipped with addition and scalar multiplication, where these operations satisfy this particular list of axioms (which are similar to the properties of these operations in $\\mathbb{R}^n$). \n\nThe elements of any vector space are called **vectors**." :tutorial/id #uuid "488db0ee-92f8-5790-8ff6-2977ab0e6dce" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3187 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185345 :tutorial/content "There are many different vector spaces. Some of the most important are listed below.\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}^n$, the set of all column vectors of height $n$ with elements from $\\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $\\text{M}_n(\\mathbb{R})$, the set of all $n \\times n$ matrices with elements from $\\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}[t]$, the set of all polynomials in the variable $t$ and with coefficients from $\\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}_n[t]$, the set of all polynomials of degree less than or equal to $n$ in the variable $t$ and with coefficients from $\\mathbb{R}$\n\n- The set of all real-valued functions of one variable." :tutorial/id #uuid "eb7bd53e-69ec-5e14-b970-1bd6ff9aff7c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6199 :tutorial/title "Important Vector Spaces"}]}
